## Issues

- [ ] **README.md:25** — Quick Start block uses `npx @aidemd-mcp/server init` without `@latest`. The spec requires `@latest` on all npx invocations ("use `npx -y @aidemd-mcp/server@latest` rather than a bare package name. The bare name uses a locally cached version if one exists"). The plan carried this omission forward (plan.aide line 34 shows `npx @aidemd-mcp/server init` without `@latest`), meaning the gap originated at planning, not implementation.
      Traces to: `.aide/readme.aide` outcomes.undesired[4] ("A config block using a bare package name without @latest, which silently serves a stale cached version to users who have previously installed any version") | Misalignment: `plan-gap`

- [ ] **`.aide/readme.aide` Context lines 81-83** — The Context paragraph hardcodes a tool count and enumerates the canonical roster: "The server currently ships six tools (`aide_discover`, `aide_read`, `aide_scaffold`, `aide_validate`, `aide_init`, `aide_upgrade`) plus a `plan.aide` file type. With six tools, full parameter documentation is feasible..." The actual surface is now eight tools — the six listed plus `aide_info` and `aide_inspect` (both registered in `src/index.ts` and surfaced through the handshake). The Context paragraph's reasoning ("six tools is small enough that every parameter can be documented... ten or fewer tools") cascades into the Strategy decision to "Document every tool with full parameters" — a decision that may or may not still hold at eight tools, but the spec gives the reader no way to know because the count is silently stale.
      Traces to:
        - `src/.aide` outcomes.undesired[6] ("A spec, test, or description that pins a specific count of tools or enumerates the tool roster as part of the contract. The count is an implementation detail of the current methodology snapshot, not an invariant — encoding it guarantees drift the moment a new lifecycle phase is added.")
        - `.aide/intent.aide` outcomes.undesired[3] ("A canonical doc that describes behavior the server does not actually deliver. Aspirational docs are worse than missing docs, because missing docs do not lie.") — the README spec's "six tools" enumeration is the reverse failure mode: the doc describes an old surface the server has since outgrown, and a downstream README written from this spec will under-document the actual tooling.
      Misalignment: `spec-gap`

- [ ] **`.aide/readme.aide` outcomes.desired[1]** — The desired outcome says "The tools section documents every tool with full parameter lists in the canonical MCP README format... so a developer evaluating the server knows exactly what capabilities they get before installing." This outcome inherits the Context's six-tool enumeration by reference: "every tool" is bounded by whatever the Context counts, and the Context counts six. Any README produced from this spec will document six tools and silently omit `aide_info` and `aide_inspect`, leaving evaluating developers unaware of two real capabilities. The drift is silent because the outcome reads as exhaustive ("every tool") while the spec internally pins the meaning of "every" to a stale count.
      Traces to:
        - `src/.aide` outcomes.desired[1] ("Every tool description is complete enough to teach the convention it invokes on its own. An agent reading only the tool list learns what `.aide` files are...") — a README that documents six of eight tools fails this principle for the two omitted tools.
        - `src/.aide` outcomes.undesired[6] (same count-pinning rule as above)
      Misalignment: `spec-gap`

### Active (alignment pass 2026-04-25 — root revision: brain-aide.md methodology doc out of sync with new schema)

The root `.aide/intent.aide` outcomes.desired commits to "`brain.aide` is a documented AIDE file type sitting alongside `intent.aide`, `plan.aide`, `todo.aide`, and `research.aide` in the canonical methodology — with its own dedicated methodology doc under `.aide/docs/` describing its schema, lifecycle, and the explicit divergence." The doc exists at `.aide/docs/brain-aide.md` but documents the prior schema (`connector`, `rootPath`, `entryFile`, `mcpServerConfig`, `tools`) with `obsidian-mcp` as the launcher and `.aide/brain.aide` as the file path. Under the new root, the schema collapses to two fields, the path moves, and the launcher renames. The doc is the canonical-doctrine surface every host project receives via `aide_init`; drift here means every new install ships stale doctrine.

- [x] `.aide/docs/brain-aide.md` Format section: renders the YAML example with all five legacy fields (`connector`, `rootPath`, `entryFile`, `mcpServerConfig` referencing `obsidian-mcp` and `${rootPath}`, `tools` block with `read`/`search`).
  Should collapse to exactly `name` + `mcpServerConfig.{command, args}` with `@bitbonsai/mcpvault` and the path inline byte-for-byte (no `${rootPath}` substitution in the default scaffold).
  Traces to: `.aide/intent.aide` outcomes.desired (frontmatter contains exactly two top-level fields: `name` + `mcpServerConfig`; default scaffold uses `@bitbonsai/mcpvault`; `mcpServerConfig.args` carries the path inline byte-for-byte).
  Misalignment: spec-gap

- [x] `.aide/docs/brain-aide.md` "Frontmatter fields" subsection: documents `connector`, `rootPath`, `entryFile`, `mcpServerConfig`, `tools` as required fields. With the schema collapse, only `name` and `mcpServerConfig.{command, args}` remain — every other field is removed. The doc must reject the legacy fields (the parser surfaces them as `malformed-frontmatter`).
  Traces to: `.aide/intent.aide` outcomes.desired (schema is `name` + `mcpServerConfig.{command, args}` only); outcomes.undesired (any frontmatter field whose only consumer is the parser's own validation is rejected).
  Misalignment: spec-gap

- [x] `.aide/docs/brain-aide.md` "Substitution surfaces" subsection: pins `${rootPath}` as the load-bearing install-time substitution case. With `rootPath` removed, the engine generalizes to any field name (currently `${name}` is the only resolvable placeholder) and the default scaffold doesn't exercise it at all.
  Traces to: `.aide/intent.aide` outcomes.desired (default scaffold makes no use of substitution; parser still supports `${...}` for advanced users who want to DRY a value across positions).
  Misalignment: spec-gap

- [x] `.aide/docs/brain-aide.md` Lifecycle subsection: describes scaffold creation at `.aide/brain.aide` (legacy path) and includes the `invalid-path` boot state in the drift-detection step. Both are stale.
  Traces to: `.aide/intent.aide` outcomes.desired (`.aide/config/brain.aide` is the canonical path; boot states reduce to four with `invalid-path` removed).
  Misalignment: spec-gap

- [x] `.aide/docs/brain-aide.md` (entire doc): every prose mention of the brain.aide path uses `.aide/brain.aide`; should update to `.aide/config/brain.aide`. The user-owned scaffold-once-never-overwritten contract for the `.aide/config/` directory should also be documented here, since the lifecycle subsection covers ownership semantics.
  Traces to: `.aide/intent.aide` outcomes.desired (`.aide/config/` is the canonical home of user-owned configuration; scaffolded once at `aide_init` if absent, then belong to the user forever).
  Misalignment: spec-gap

- [x] `.aide/handoff.aide` ("Design decisions" #1): "`brain.aide` is the 6th AIDE file type. Lives at `.aide/brain.aide`. Schema: `connector`, `rootPath`, `entryFile`, `mcpServerConfig`, `tools` + a `## Prose` body. No `scope`/`outcomes`/`status` fields."
  Stale path and stale schema. handoff.aide is a session-log artifact, not an authoritative spec, but it's the file a cold-start agent reads to orient on the prior session's decisions. Letting it carry the old schema means future agents reorient on outdated state. The handoff should be updated to reflect the post-revision schema and path, with the old schema labeled as superseded.
  Traces to: `.aide/intent.aide` outcomes.desired (path move; schema collapse; field rename).
  Misalignment: spec-gap
  **Resolved:** the handoff was rewritten end-to-end before the schema-collapse build cycle. The current handoff has no "Design decisions" section; its body documents the new schema, the new path, and the four-state vocabulary throughout.

- [x] `.aide/handoff.aide` followup #3: "Package name on the launcher: `obsidian-mcp` vs `@bitbonsai/mcpvault`." This followup is now resolved by the root revision (the new default IS `@bitbonsai/mcpvault`). The handoff entry should be marked resolved or removed; leaving it as an open followup misleads future agents into thinking the question is still live.
  Traces to: `.aide/intent.aide` outcomes.desired (default launcher is `@bitbonsai/mcpvault`).
  Misalignment: spec-gap
  **Resolved:** the handoff was rewritten end-to-end before the schema-collapse build cycle. The current handoff has no "followups" section; the launcher question is settled and reflected throughout the new content.

## Retro

The first issue (Quick Start `@latest`) is plan-level: the planner read the
`@latest` requirement as scoped only to JSON config blocks and did not apply
it to the `npx @aidemd-mcp/server init` CLI command. That retro is preserved
unchanged from the prior QA pass.

The second and third issues are spec-level alignment drift that should have
been caught when `aide_info` and `aide_inspect` were added to the server. The
exact same drift pattern is already flagged at `src/todo.aide` against
`src/.aide` outcomes.desired[0] — the count and roster enumeration drift hit
two specs simultaneously, but the realignment fix landed in neither. The
README spec's Context paragraph and outcomes.desired[1] share the same blast
radius: any new tool added to the surface silently invalidates both the
count and the implicit "every tool" scope without any signal to the spec
author.

What would have caught this earlier:
- An aide-aligner pass after every `src/index.ts` registration change. The
  prior QA log on `src/.aide` already names this gap ("Any commit that adds
  a new `case` to the `CallToolRequestSchema` handler in `src/index.ts`
  should trigger a review of `src/.aide` outcomes.desired[0]") — the
  alignment cascade up to `.aide/readme.aide` was missed because no agent
  walked beyond the immediate parent.
- A spec-writer rule against numeric tool counts and roster enumerations
  in any spec under the project. The root `.aide/intent.aide` and parent
  `src/.aide` both forbid these encodings; the README spec inherited the
  rule but encoded the exact pattern the rule warns against. The aligner
  needs to catch these inheritance violations on every alignment pass.

### Active cycle (2026-04-25 — root revision: methodology doc + handoff out of sync)

The root `.aide/intent.aide` revision settled five interlocking decisions (path move to `.aide/config/`, launcher rename, schema collapse, `connector` → `name`, `invalid-path` removal) that cascade through every spec downstream. The methodology doc `.aide/docs/brain-aide.md` is the canonical-doctrine surface that ships with `aide_init` and teaches every host project the file format — it must agree with the root, but it currently documents the prior schema.

The handoff at `.aide/handoff.aide` describes a refactor that COMPLETED with the prior schema, so its design-decisions block and its followups are now historically accurate-but-superseded. Future cold-start agents reading the handoff will reorient on outdated state unless the handoff is updated to flag the supersession.

This is the same root-architectural-shifts pattern from `feedback_root_architectural_shifts.md`: a major root revision leaves the trunk surfaces (handoff, methodology docs) un-updated, and the drift accumulates as peer-level violations no single leaf carries.

What would have caught this earlier: `/aide:align` should follow root revisions immediately, before any handoff or methodology-doc consumer can re-cite the old shape. The methodology doc's load-bearing role (every `aide_init` ships it) makes this drift especially expensive — it propagates to every new host install on the next release.
